      "text": "1. We start with the given equation of the hyperbola:\n\\[\n\\frac{y^2}{27} - \\frac{x^2}{11} = 1\n\\]\n\n2. To standardize the form of the hyperbola, we make a translation to place the center at the origin. For the equation to be in standard form \\(\\frac{y^2}{a^2} - \\frac{x^2}{b^2} = 1\\), we complete the square for \\(y\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{y^2 - 27}{27} - \\frac{x^2}{11} = 1\n\\]\n\\[\ny^2 - 27 = 27 \\left( \\frac{y^2}{27} - \\frac{x^2}{11} \\right)\n\\]\n\\[\ny^2 - 27 = \\frac{y^2}{9} - \\frac{x^2}{11}\n\\]\n\n3. Solving for \\(y^2\\):\n\\[\n9y^2 - 243 = y^2 - x^2\n\\]\n\\[\n8y^2 = x^2 + 243\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{y^2}{\\frac{243}{8}} - \\frac{x^2}{243} = 1\n\\]\n\n4. We now recognize that the standard form of the hyperbola is:\n\\[\n\\frac{y^2}{a^2} - \\frac{x^2}{b^2} = 1\n\\]\nComparing with the given equation, we see that:\n\\[\na^2 = 27 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b^2 = 11\n\\]\n\n5. For hyperbolas of the form \\(\\frac{y^2}{a^2} - \\frac{x^2}{b^2} = 1\\), the distance between the vertices is \\(2a\\). Thus, we calculate:\n\\[\n2a = 2 \\sqrt{27}\n\\]\n\\[\n2a = 2 \\cdot \\sqrt{27}\n\\]\n\\[\n2a = 2 \\cdot 3\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\\[\n2a = 6\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\nConclusion: The distance between the vertices of the hyperbola is\n\\[\n\\boxed{6\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]",
